8: cite30†Image cite31†Xinyu Wang ,\nL69: \nL70: [Button: Xiaobin Wang] ,\nL71: \nL72: cite32†Image cite33†Liangcai Su ,\nL73: \nL74: [Button: Zhen Zhang] ,\nL75: [Button: Shibin Wu] ,\nL76: \nL77: [Button: Zhengwei Tao] ,\nL78: \nL79: [Button: Yong Jiang] ,\nL80: \nL81: [Button: Pengjun Xie] ,\nL82: \nL83: [Button: Fei Huang] ,\nL84: \nL85: [Button: Jingren Zhou]\nL86: ## Abstract\nL87: \nL88: A scalable framework and two-phase fine-tuning strategy enhance function-calling capabilities of agents in diverse environments, improving performance on agentic benchmarks.\nL89: \nL90: Generated by cite34†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L91: Advanced cite35†agentic intelligence is a prerequisite for deploying Large Language Models in practical, real-world applications. Diverse real-world APIs demand precise, robust cite36†function-calling intelligence , which needs agents to develop these capabilities through interaction in varied environments. The breadth of function-calling competence is closely tied to the diversity of environments in which agents are trained.\nL92: In this work, we scale up environments as a step towards advancing general cite35†agentic intelligence . This gives rise to two central challenges: (i) how to scale environments in a principled manner, and (ii) how to effectively train agentic capabilities from experiences derived through interactions with these environments.\nL93: To address these, we design a scalable framework that automatically constructs cite37†heterogeneous environments that are fully simulated, systematically broadening the space of function-calling scenarios. We further adapt a cite38†two-phase agent fine-tuning strategy: first endowing agents with fundamental agentic capabilities, then specializing them for domain-specific contexts.\nL94: Extensive experiments on agentic benchmarks, cite39†tau-bench , cite40†tau2-Bench , and cite41†ACEBench , demonstrate that our trained model, cite42†AgentScaler , significantly enhances the function-calling capability of models.\nL95: cite43†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite44†View PDF†arxiv.org cite27†Add to collection L96: ### Community\nL97: \nL98: cite45†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L99: \nL100: cite26†taesiri L101: \nL102: Paper submitter cite46†Sep 17, 2025 L103: Advanced agentic intelligence is a prerequisite for deploying Large Language Models in practical, real-world applications. Diverse real-world APIs demand precise, robust function-calling intelligence, which needs agents to develop these capabilities through interaction in varied environments. The breadth of function-calling competence is closely tied to the diversity of environments in which agents are trained. In this work, we scale up environments as a step towards advancing general agentic intelligence.\nL104: This gives rise to two central challenges: (i) how to scale environments in a principled manner, and (ii) how to effectively train agentic capabilities from experiences derived through interactions with these environments. To address these, we design a scalable framework that automatically constructs heterogeneous environments that are fully simulated, systematically broadening the space of function-calling scenarios.\nL105: We further adapt a two-phase agent fine-tuning strategy: first endowing agents with fundamental agentic capabilities, then specializing them for domain-specific contexts. Extensive experiments on agentic benchmarks, tau-bench, tau2-Bench, and ACEBench, demonstrate that our trained model, AgentScaler, significantly enhances the function-calling capability of models.\nL106: Reply\nL107: \nL108: cite45†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L109: \nL110: cite26†taesiri L111: \nL112: Paper submitter cite47†Sep 17, 2025 L113: \nL114: Github: cite48†https://github.com/Alibaba-NLP/DeepResearch†github.com L115: Blog: cite49†https://tongyi-agent.github.io/blog/†tongyi-agent.github.io L116: \nL117:   *   * 